  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Vararia investiens is a wood-decay fungus belonging to the Lachnocladiaceae family, characterized by its corticioid (crust-like) fruiting bodies. This species plays an important ecological role as a decomposer in forest ecosystems throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Crust-like Texture",
      "description": "Forms thin, adherent crusts on wood surfaces rather than fruiting bodies with distinct caps or stems",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Hymenial Surface",
      "description": "Features a finely granular or slightly wrinkled hymenial (spore-bearing) surface that is typically whitish to pale tan in color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate Preference",
      "description": "Grows on hardwood logs and branches, particularly on recently fallen or moist wood",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Vararia investiens thrives on decaying hardwood substrates in moist, forested environments throughout New England, commonly found on fallen logs and dead branches on the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a corticioid fungus, Vararia investiens lacks the elaborate fruiting structures of many other fungi, instead spreading its spores directly from a thin crust of tissue barely visible to the naked eye.",
